Damien Neil wrote on Wed, 05 Jan 2005 13:25:38 -0800:
> In article <MPG.1c40ac22cc9e4ea698a2cf@216.168.3.44>, Rast
> <rast2@hotmail.com> wrote:
> > Damien Neil wrote on Thu, 16 Dec 2004 16:09:14 -0800:

> I don't think anyone trying for a high score should feel compelled to
> play an illiterate vegan atheist.  It feels like a distraction from the
> core game.

I agree; if a player is trying for _a_ high score, "all" he needs to do 
is ascend, and he'll have one of the highest scores.

But if he is trying for _the_ high score, than yes, he _should_ feel 
compelled to ascend an illiterate vegan atheist, and any other conducts 
he can keep, and all as fast as possible too.  There simply haven't been 
any other suggestions for skill testing behavior besides ascending 
quickly and ascending with conducts.  Exploration score, "carnage score", 
wealth, "Should I try to kill off a few more demon lords, or am I better 
off sprinting for the exit?" -- none of these things are skill testing.


> I'm very dubious about this.  What's harder--an illiterate or a vegan? 
> What if it's an illiterate wizard vs. a vegan monk?

The illiterate wizard is harder.  But let's not consider class 
differences;  if one class is better than another at getting high scores, 
than so be it.  We already know that some classes are better than others 
at ascending.

Illiterate vs vegan -- Vegan is no picnic (ha!) for a non-monk, but I'd 
say illiterate looks a lot harder. 


> I can see the argument that speed is the only worthwhile measure of
> ascention skill.  It's certainly better than the current scoring
> system.  The problem is that it's uninteresting--you don't need a score
> counter at all, just a turns counter. 

It is quite interesting, because it is challenging.

> Another problem is that using a
> single metric gives more weight to the RNG: The person who finds
> Stormbringer or a wand of wishing on level 1 is given a dramatic
> advantage.  (They get a dramatic advantage under any circumstances, of
> course, but I believe a turns-only system increases it.)

I'm hardpressed to come up with any score system where finding a wand of 
wishing does not provide an advantage in the final score.

Other than, of course, the current system: if you don't die, all that 
matters is how many times you cast create monster.  



